/**

@page page_glossary_home Glossary

@tableofcontents

This page collects short, precise definitions of terms that recur throughout
Peano's documentation. Where a term originates from a particular publication,
the entry says so and points to the source.




# Events and action sets {#glossary_events_and_action_sets}

As a traversal runs through the mesh and reports on the steps that it makes
to the @ref glossary_observer "observer", the observer creates
events : touchVertexFirstTime, touchVertexLastTime, touchFaceFirstTime, touchFaceLastTime,
and so forth. Events are a logical concepts.

Behaviour can now be injected into the traversal by actually doing something
whenever an event arises. Technically, this means that we write a function 
which does something whenever the event touchFaceFirstTime for example pops
up. Unsurprisingly, we call this function touchFaceFirstTime() and we sometimes
refer to it as **action** tied to an **event**.

All the actions, i.e. functions, are collected in an action set. An action set
is simply a collection of functions for the events that can arise if we run
through a mesh. Each action set class is tied to an observer. The observer
creates the events, i.e. triggers the functions (actions) from the action set.
Observers can hold multiple action sets and trigger their functions one by 
one or in parallel - depending on the action set spec.

These concepts are all mapped 1:1 onto C++, but there are also Python abstractions:
The Python class peano4.solversteps.ActionSet consdequently is where a user or
extension implements what should actually happen for some of these events.

@ref page_peano_action_sets "Action sets and events" for the full
discussion of all possible actions, including the order in which multiple action sets'
events are triggered for the same mesh entity and how to control their order
and parallel execution.

The object diagram below illustrates the collaboration for a single event: a
@ref glossary_spacetree "spacetree" calls traverse() with a TraversalObserver
instance, which - while it runs through the mesh - dispatches the resulting
event function calls onto the action set instances it holds.

\dot
digraph EventsAndActionSets {
  rankdir=LR
  fontname="Helvetica"
  node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fillcolor=white, fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10]
  edge [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=9]

  Spacetree  [label=<<U>aSpacetree : Spacetree</U>>]
  Observer   [label=<<U>anObserver : TraversalObserver</U>>, fillcolor=lightyellow]
  ActionSetA [label=<<U>reconstructPatch : ActionSet</U>>]
  ActionSetB [label=<<U>plotSolution : ActionSet</U>>]

  Spacetree  -> Observer   [label="traverse(anObserver)"]
  Observer   -> ActionSetA [label="touchCellFirstTime()\ntouchVertexLastTime()\n..."]
  Observer   -> ActionSetB [label="touchCellFirstTime()\ntouchVertexLastTime()\n..."]
}
\enddot

The example above illustrates the core concepts:

- There is a spacetree. When we say traverse, we tell the spacetree which observer to use.
- The spacetree's traverse() informs this observer object of state transitions (e.g. going from one cell to another or ascending/descending in the tree).
- The observer manages some data movements and then triggers events (e.g. "now I touch a face for the very first time").
- The observer is tied to action sets. In the example there are two of them, one called reconstructPatch and the other one plotSolution.
- Per event, it calls the actions (functions) in each action sets, which now actually do what the user intends the traversal to do.


# Observer (algorithmic step) {#glossary_observer}

Peano's fundamental idea is that there are @ref glossary_spacetree "spacetrees" and the user calls Peano
to run through these spacetrees. Whenever Peano starts traversing a spacetree,
it employs an **observer**. Logically, an **Observer** can also be called an 
**algorithmic step** - it is tied to one full, parallel traversal
of the mesh ().


As Peano runs through the mesh, it "tells" the observer what mesh transitions
is performs. The observer then can move around the data as it sees fit, i.e.
load face or cell data from input streams, write data into MPI buffers, and so 
forth. The observer can also trigger events.


Each observer holds a set of action sets. If the observer identifies an event,
these events are forwarded to the action sets, which then map them onto actions.
This terminology can be simplified dramatically.



# Spacetree {#glossary_spacetree}

A **spacetree** is the tree-based adaptive Cartesian mesh data structure
underlying Peano. The term is used as a generalisation of the classical
quadtree (2D) and octree (3D) data structures to arbitrary dimension @f$
d \geq 2 @f$ and to partitionings other than bipartitioning.

The computational domain is scaled and dilated to fit into a unit hypercube
of dimension @f$ d @f$. This hypercube is cut into @f$ k @f$ equidistant
slices along each coordinate axis, yielding @f$ k^d @f$ small cubes that form
a regular Cartesian grid; the cut also defines a parent-child relation
between the newly created cubes and the cube they were cut from. This cutting
is continued recursively and independently for each new cube. The process
terminates in a set of cubes @f$ \mathcal{T} @f$ whose leaves, i.e. the cubes
that are not refined any further, span an adaptive Cartesian grid, while the
parent-child relation (or its inverse) imposes a hierarchy, i.e. a tree, on
@f$ \mathcal{T} @f$.

Equivalently, if the level @f$ \ell @f$ of a tree node is defined as the
length of the path from the root (the original bounding box) to that node,
the nodes of one level span a regular grid @f$ \Omega_{h,\ell} @f$, and the
adaptive grid as a whole is @f$ \Omega_h = \bigcup_\ell \Omega_{h,\ell} @f$.
Tree language and grid language for spacetrees are therefore equivalent, once
an adaptive Cartesian grid is read as a composite of disconnected regular
Cartesian grids of different resolution, embedded into each other.

Peano tripartitions, i.e. it uses @f$ k = 3 @f$, and enumerates/linearises
the cells of a spacetree along a Peano space-filling curve rather than via
plain depth-first or breadth-first search, since a space-filling-curve-based
enumeration turns the tree's parent-child partial order into a total order
and yields, among other properties, face-connected traversal steps.

@image html documentation/glossary-spacetree-figure1.png "Figure 1 from Weinzierl (2019): construction of a spacetree from repeated regular refinement of a square, and the resulting Peano space-filling curve traversal."

<i>Figure reproduced from: T. Weinzierl, "The Peano Software&mdash;Parallel,
Automaton-based, Dynamically Adaptive Grid Traversals," ACM Transactions on
Mathematical Software, 45(2), Article 14, 2019,
<a href="https://doi.org/10.1145/3319797">doi:10.1145/3319797</a>
(<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1506.04496">arXiv:1506.04496</a>).</i>

The four panels of the figure, left to right, show: the computational domain
embedded into a square; the Peano space-filling curve motif inducing an
ordering on the @f$ 3 \times 3 @f$ Cartesian grid obtained from one
refinement step of that square; the same construction after a further
refinement step, still following the Peano curve; and, on top, an adaptively
refined Cartesian grid with, below it, the corresponding spacetree.


*/
